.SH "The Fastest Directory Crawler & Globber for NodeJS"
.PP

.PP

.PP
\fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP 

.PP
⚡ \fBThe Fastest:\fP Nothing similar (in the NodeJS world) beats \fRfdir\fP in speed\&. It can easily crawl a directory containing \fB1 million files in < 1 second\&.\fP

.PP
💡 \fBStupidly Easy:\fP \fRfdir\fP uses expressive Builder pattern to build the crawler increasing code readability\&.

.PP
🤖 \fBZero Dependencies*:\fP \fRfdir\fP only uses NodeJS \fRfs\fP & \fRpath\fP modules\&.

.PP
🕺 \fBAstonishingly Small:\fP < 2KB in size gzipped & minified\&.

.PP
🖮 \fBHackable:\fP Extending \fRfdir\fP is extremely simple now that the new Builder API is here\&. Feel free to experiment around\&.

.PP
_* \fRpicomatch\fP must be installed manually by the user to support globbing\&._
.SS "🚄 Quickstart"
.SS "Installation"
You can install using \fRnpm\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm i fdir
.fi
.PP

.PP
or Yarn:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ yarn add fdir
.fi
.PP
.SS "Usage"
.PP
.nf
import { fdir } from "fdir";

// create the builder
const api = new fdir()\&.withFullPaths()\&.crawl("path/to/dir");

// get all files in a directory synchronously
const files = api\&.sync();

// or asynchronously
api\&.withPromise()\&.then((files) => {
  // do something with the result here\&.
});
.fi
.PP
